A||||4||FieroMike||E-Brake rubbing?
Z||000000||FieroMike||04-13-99||09:42 AM||||Ever take a look underneath your car and follow your e-brake lines?  Mine runs between the lower control arm bracket and the bottom of the boot on the drive axle.  They are both rubbing and the rubber is worn through on the e-brake line.  To me it looks like the boots have been replaced before!  Ever notice this?  I drilled holes through the control arm and ran it through the other side, this is simple and they should have done it in the factory! It even lines up better. Take a look.
Z||000001||theogre||04-13-99||12:51 PM||||this actually was the subject of one of the recalls on the car. I foget what years were affected.<p>I had noticed clearance on mine was low, but it doesn't apear to be hitting anything. I'll look at it a bit better if I ever get a dry weekend.<p>Personally I don't recomend everyone drilling into a control arm unless you know what you're doing. you could severly weaken the arm. I agree the cables could use some work on allot of cars, but I think I'd like to find an alternate to drilling if posible. Not everyone is comfortable making that kind of alteration. 
Z||000002||FieroMike||04-13-99||01:11 PM||||theogre is right, I wasn't telling anybody to drill through their control arm, sorry.  I actually drilled through a really thin plate that sits behind the arm (but is welded on) I wouldn't recommend drilling anything ON the control arm.  Any more information on that recall would be appreciated!  I think if you bend it to turn towards the front of the car BEFORE the arm you might get it to work, but the linkage to the brake is behind the arm.
Z||000003||PaUL||04-15-99||02:06 PM||||FieroMike,<br>When I got the parking brake recall on my car done, an 86SC the only thing they replaced were the pistons for the rear calipers. They didnt touch the cables. I took my rear calipers apart, the new pistons have a massive spring behind them to make the rear brakes self adjust. The old ones didnt. If your rear pistons have a small yellow plastic plug on the front, you have the old flawed design and the parking brake will never work properly. 
Z||000004||Jim85SC||05-05-99||07:40 AM||||I had this problem once after a caliper rebuild.  As I recall, the bracket that holds the end of the cable housing can be rotated (if the right bolt is loosened?).  If this bracket is rotated too far up, the cable rubs on the boot.
